{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts | sed -n '60,175p'\"","aggregated_output":"    60\t  \"thinking_level_select\",\n    61\t  \"session_before_fork\",\n    62\t  \"session_before_switch\",\n    63\t  \"session_shutdown\",\n    64\t] as const satisfies readonly PiHookName[];\n    65\t\n    66\texport type Epic1Hook = (typeof EPIC_1_HOOKS)[number];\n    67\t\n    68\t/** What the connector needs from the host to run a session. Every dependency\n    69\t *  has a working production default; the overrides exist for test isolation\n    70\t *  (temp registry/thread, scripted launch/selection) and for Stories 5/6 to swap\n    71\t *  in the PI-auth-backed inference config.\n    72\t *\n    73\t *  `buildSdkConfig` returns the SdkConfig the instance is constructed from. Its\n    74\t *  default is the observe-only config below (a real, construction-valid SDK);\n    75\t *  Stories 5/6 inject the PI-model-registry-backed `ModelCall` + assignments. */\n    76\texport interface ConnectorDeps {\n    77\t  buildSdkConfig?: (ctx: ExtensionContext) => OpResult<SdkConfig>;\n    78\t  registryPath?: string;\n    79\t  newThreadFilePath?: () => string;\n    80\t  parseLaunch?: () => LaunchFlags;\n    81\t  selectThread?: (\n    82\t    choices: readonly ThreadChoice[],\n    83\t    ctx: ExtensionContext,\n    84\t  ) => Promise<string | null>;\n    85\t}\n    86\t\n    87\t/** Everything the connector retains across hooks as PLAIN data — by\n    88\t *  construction structuredClone-able. The live `LhcInstance` is held separately\n    89\t *  in the closure (it is the connector's own engine, rebuilt on reload) and is\n    90\t *  deliberately not part of the snapshot. */\n    91\texport interface ConnectorSnapshot {\n    92\t  state: SessionState | null;\n    93\t  lastDiagnostic: CaptureFailureDiagnostic | null;\n    94\t}\n    95\t\n    96\texport interface Connector {\n    97\t  /** Register the observe-only hook rail on the PI extension API. */\n    98\t  register(pi: ExtensionAPI): void;\n    99\t  /** The plain-data state retained across hooks. `null` until a session\n   100\t   *  resolves a thread; never holds a PI ctx. */\n   101\t  getState(): SessionState | null;\n   102\t  /** The live LHC instance for the current session, or `null`. A test/inspection\n   103\t   *  seam — held by the connector but never part of the plain-data snapshot. */\n   104\t  getInstance(): LhcInstance | null;\n   105\t  /** Plain-data snapshot of all retained state (inspection/test seam). */\n   106\t  snapshot(): ConnectorSnapshot;\n   107\t  /** The hook handlers, keyed by event — exposed so tests can drive them with\n   108\t   *  synthetic ctx/events without a live PI. */\n   109\t  readonly handlers: Readonly<Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>>>;\n   110\t}\n   111\t\n   112\t/** The connector's live capture state for one session: the open-turn\n   113\t *  accumulator plus the stable PI session id used to scope idempotency keys.\n   114\t *  Held in the closure alongside the live `LhcInstance` — engine state rebuilt\n   115\t *  per session, never part of the plain-data snapshot and never a PI object. */\n   116\tinterface CaptureSession {\n   117\t  piSessionId: string;\n   118\t  accumulator: TurnAccumulator;\n   119\t  sourceSeq: number;\n   120\t}\n   121\t\n   122\t/** Fork state captured from `session_before_fork` and used on the next\n   123\t *  `session_start{fork}` to create and seed the forked thread. Plain data\n   124\t *  only — no PI objects retained across the fork boundary. */\n   125\tinterface PendingFork {\n   126\t  sourceThreadRef: ThreadRef;\n   127\t  forkEntryId: string;\n   128\t}\n   129\t\n   130\t/** A stable per-session id for idempotency-key scoping — the resolved thread's\n   131\t *  identity, which survives reload/replay (the same thread re-resolves to the\n   132\t *  same id). */\n   133\tfunction sessionIdOf(ref: ThreadRef): string {\n   134\t  return \"threadId\" in ref ? ref.threadId : ref.filePath;\n   135\t}\n   136\t\n   137\tconst DEFAULT_LHC_DIR = join(homedir(), \".lhc\");\n   138\t\n   139\t/** The Story-1 production inference placeholder: a real `DerivationProvider`\n   140\t *  whose every operation fails closed (non-retryable) instead of fabricating a\n   141\t *  derived form. Epic 1 is observe-only and Story 1 does not capture yet\n   142\t *  (message_end is a no-op until Story 2) and lands no work handlers, so no\n   143\t *  derivation work is ever queued and this provider is never invoked — it exists\n   144\t *  only to satisfy SDK construction (AC-1.1 \"initializes one LHC instance\").\n   145\t *\n   146\t *  It is deliberately NOT the deterministic provider, which the LHC SDK\n   147\t *  documents as \"selectable only by explicit name — never a production default\"\n   148\t *  (it would mark fake derived text into the thread). Failing closed preserves\n   149\t *  the observe-only guarantee even if a future change queued work before Stories\n   150\t *  5/6 wire the real PI-model-registry-backed inference config (`ModelCall` +\n   151\t *  assignments), which is what a real *completing* inference requires (the\n   152\t *  `@earendil-works/pi-ai` completion package is not yet a build dependency). */\n   153\tfunction observeOnlyProvider(): DerivationProvider {\n   154\t  const notConfigured = (): Promise<ProviderResult> =>\n   155\t    Promise.resolve({\n   156\t      ok: false,\n   157\t      retryable: false,\n   158\t      reason: \"pi-lhc inference is not configured yet (Epic 1 Stories 5/6)\",\n   159\t    });\n   160\t  return {\n   161\t    smoothPrompt: notConfigured,\n   162\t    summarizeToolCall: notConfigured,\n   163\t    summarizeToolResult: notConfigured,\n   164\t    composeTurnRendering: notConfigured,\n   165\t    projectLowerBand: notConfigured,\n   166\t    summarizeChunkDetailed: notConfigured,\n   167\t    summarizeChunkBrief: notConfigured,\n   168\t  };\n   169\t}\n   170\t\n   171\t/** The production observe-only SDK config: a real, construction-valid instance\n   172\t *  in background scheduler mode, built on the fail-closed `observeOnlyProvider`\n   173\t *  above. Stories 5/6 replace this default with the PI-auth-backed inference\n   174\t *  config. */\n   175\tfunction defaultBuildSdkConfig(): OpResult<SdkConfig> {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/model-call.ts | sed -n '1,170p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t// AC-4.1..4.4. The host-supplied ModelCall function.\n     2\t//\n     3\t// Resolves (provider,model) via ctx.modelRegistry.find, completes through\n     4\t// pi-ai's complete(), and classifies failures into the LHC contract.\n     5\t// Provider/model are opaque routing keys — the connector does not interpret them.\n     6\t//\n     7\t// NOTE: The real pi-ai package is not yet a build dependency. This implementation\n     8\t// uses a local placeholder that simulates the pi-ai complete() contract. The\n     9\t// production swap is mechanical when the dependency lands (Spec Deviation).\n    10\t\n    11\timport type { ModelCall, ModelCallFailureKind, ModelCallResult } from \"lhc\";\n    12\timport type { ExtensionContext, ModelHandle } from \"../pi/types.js\";\n    13\timport type { CompleteContext, PiAiComplete, PiAiError } from \"./pi-ai.js\";\n    14\t\n    15\t/** Extract the text content from an AssistantMessage. */\n    16\tfunction extractAssistantText(message: { content?: ReadonlyArray<{ type: string; text?: string }> }): string {\n    17\t  const content = message.content ?? [];\n    18\t  const textParts = content.filter((part) => part.type === \"text\" && part.text !== undefined);\n    19\t  return textParts.map((part) => part.text as string).join(\"\\n\");\n    20\t}\n    21\t\n    22\t/** Pure mapping of a PI/pi-ai failure shape to a ModelCallFailureKind\n    23\t *  (terminal: auth, invalid_request; retryable: rate_limit, timeout, network;\n    24\t *  thrown → other). */\n    25\texport function classifyFailure(err: unknown): ModelCallFailureKind {\n    26\t  // Guard: non-object errors fall through to \"other\"\n    27\t  if (typeof err !== \"object\" || err === null) {\n    28\t    return \"other\";\n    29\t  }\n    30\t\n    31\t  const errorLike = err as Partial<PiAiError & Error>;\n    32\t  const code = errorLike.code?.toLowerCase() ?? \"\";\n    33\t  const type = errorLike.type?.toLowerCase() ?? \"\";\n    34\t  const message = errorLike.message?.toLowerCase() ?? \"\";\n    35\t\n    36\t  // Check for auth failures (terminal)\n    37\t  const authPatterns = [\"auth\", \"unauthorized\", \"401\", \"authentication\"];\n    38\t  if (authPatterns.some((p) => code.includes(p) || type.includes(p) || message.includes(p))) {\n    39\t    return \"auth\";\n    40\t  }\n    41\t\n    42\t  // Check for invalid request (terminal)\n    43\t  const invalidPatterns = [\"invalid\", \"400\", \"bad\"];\n    44\t  if (invalidPatterns.some((p) => code.includes(p) || type.includes(p) || message.includes(p))) {\n    45\t    return \"invalid_request\";\n    46\t  }\n    47\t\n    48\t  // Check for rate limit (retryable)\n    49\t  const rateLimitPatterns = [\"rate\", \"429\"];\n    50\t  if (rateLimitPatterns.some((p) => code.includes(p) || type.includes(p) || message.includes(p))) {\n    51\t    return \"rate_limit\";\n    52\t  }\n    53\t\n    54\t  // Check for timeout (retryable)\n    55\t  const timeoutPatterns = [\"timeout\", \"timed\", \"408\"];\n    56\t  if (timeoutPatterns.some((p) => code.includes(p) || type.includes(p) || message.includes(p))) {\n    57\t    return \"timeout\";\n    58\t  }\n    59\t\n    60\t  // Check for network errors (retryable)\n    61\t  const networkPatterns = [\"network\", \"connection\", \"econnrefused\", \"enotfound\", \"fetch\"];\n    62\t  if (networkPatterns.some((p) => code.includes(p) || type.includes(p) || message.includes(p))) {\n    63\t    return \"network\";\n    64\t  }\n    65\t\n    66\t  // Default for unknown errors\n    67\t  return \"other\";\n    68\t}\n    69\t\n    70\t/** Simulated pi-ai complete() for when the real package is not available.\n    71\t *  This will be replaced with the real import when the dependency lands. */\n    72\tfunction mockComplete(model: string, context: CompleteContext): Promise<{ content?: Array<{ type: string; text?: string }> }> {\n    73\t  // In production, this would be:\n    74\t  // import { complete } from \"@earendil-works/pi-ai\";\n    75\t  // return complete(model, context);\n    76\t  //\n    77\t  // For now, return a placeholder that simulates the response shape.\n    78\t  // The real implementation swaps in the actual import mechanically.\n    79\t  return Promise.resolve({\n    80\t    content: [{ type: \"text\", text: \"(pi-ai not yet integrated - placeholder response)\" }],\n    81\t  });\n    82\t}\n    83\t\n    84\t/** Build the host ModelCall function.\n    85\t *\n    86\t * The function:\n    87\t * - Resolves (provider, model) via ctx.modelRegistry.find()\n    88\t * - Completes via pi-ai's complete()\n    89\t * - Classifies failures into ModelCallFailureKind\n    90\t * - Never returns \"empty_output\" (that's LHC's adapter's job)\n    91\t *\n    92\t * AC-4.1: Resolves provider/model and returns completion text.\n    93\t * AC-4.2: Different derivation kinds route to different pairs.\n    94\t * AC-4.3: Provider errors map to LHC failure classifications.\n    95\t * AC-4.4: Host function never returns empty_output.\n    96\t */\n    97\texport function createModelCall(ctx: ExtensionContext): ModelCall {\n    98\t  return async ({ provider, model, messages }): Promise<ModelCallResult> => {\n    99\t    // Resolve the model through PI's registry\n   100\t    const resolved = ctx.modelRegistry.find(provider, model);\n   101\t    if (resolved === undefined) {\n   102\t      // Unknown model - treat as invalid request (terminal)\n   103\t      return {\n   104\t        ok: false,\n   105\t        kind: \"invalid_request\",\n   106\t        message: `Model not found in registry: ${provider}/${model}`,\n   107\t      };\n   108\t    }\n   109\t\n   110\t    // Check if auth is configured\n   111\t    if (!ctx.modelRegistry.hasConfiguredAuth(resolved)) {\n   112\t      // No auth - treat as auth failure (terminal)\n   113\t      return {\n   114\t        ok: false,\n   115\t        kind: \"auth\",\n   116\t        message: `No configured auth for model: ${provider}/${model}`,\n   117\t      };\n   118\t    }\n   119\t\n   120\t    // Complete through pi-ai\n   121\t    try {\n   122\t      const completeContext: CompleteContext = { messages };\n   123\t      const response = await mockComplete(model, completeContext);\n   124\t\n   125\t      // Extract text from the response\n   126\t      const text = extractAssistantText(response);\n   127\t\n   128\t      // NOTE: We do NOT check for empty text here. AC-4.4 specifies that\n   129\t      // empty_output is classified by LHC's adapter, not by the host function.\n   130\t      // The host function returns text-or-failure only, even if the text is empty.\n   131\t      return { ok: true, text };\n   132\t    } catch (err) {\n   133\t      // Classify the error into a ModelCallFailureKind\n   134\t      const kind = classifyFailure(err);\n   135\t      const message = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n   136\t      return { ok: false, kind, message };\n   137\t    }\n   138\t  };\n   139\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba packages/pi-lhc/test/inference/closed-loop.test.ts | sed -n '60,145p'\"","aggregated_output":"    60\t\n    61\t  afterEach(async () => {\n    62\t    if (connector !== null && threadRef !== null) {\n    63\t      try {\n    64\t        await connector.handlers.session_shutdown(ctx, { reason: \"shutdown\" });\n    65\t      } catch (e) {\n    66\t        // Ignore cleanup errors in tests\n    67\t        console.warn(\"Cleanup error:\", e);\n    68\t      }\n    69\t    }\n    70\t    store.cleanup();\n    71\t  });\n    72\t\n    73\t  describe(\"TC-4.5: Capture → derivation → recorded-outcome loop\", () => {\n    74\t    it(\"captured thread's queued derivation invokes function and persists ready form + classified failure\", async () => {\n    75\t      // Simplified test: use a ModelCall that returns text for all calls\n    76\t      // This proves the loop works end-to-end without complex routing\n    77\t      const modelCall = fakeModelCallText(\"derived summary\");\n    78\t\n    79\t      const assignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment> = {\n    80\t        smoothed_prompt: { provider: \"openai\", model: \"gpt-5.4\", prompt: DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES.smoothed_prompt },\n    81\t        tool_call_summary: { provider: \"openai\", model: \"gpt-5.4\", prompt: DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES.tool_call_summary },\n    82\t        tool_result_summary: { provider: \"openai\", model: \"gpt-5.4\", prompt: DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES.tool_result_summary },\n    83\t        turn_rendering: { provider: \"openai\", model: \"gpt-5.4\", prompt: DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES.turn_rendering },\n    84\t        lower_band_projection: { provider: \"openai\", model: \"gpt-5.4\", prompt: DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES.lower_band_projection },\n    85\t        chunk_summary_detailed: { provider: \"openai\", model: \"gpt-5.4\", prompt: DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES.chunk_summary_detailed },\n    86\t        chunk_summary_brief: { provider: \"openai\", model: \"gpt-5.4\", prompt: DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES.chunk_summary_brief },\n    87\t      };\n    88\t\n    89\t      const inferenceConfig: InferenceConfig = {\n    90\t        call: modelCall,\n    91\t        assignments,\n    92\t      };\n    93\t\n    94\t      const sdkConfig: SdkConfig = {\n    95\t        inference: inferenceConfig,\n    96\t        mode: \"background\",\n    97\t      };\n    98\t\n    99\t      // Create connector with our inference config\n   100\t      connector = createConnector({\n   101\t        registryPath: store.registryPath,\n   102\t        newThreadFilePath: () => store.threadPath(),\n   103\t        buildSdkConfig: () => ({ ok: true, value: sdkConfig }),\n   104\t      });\n   105\t\n   106\t      // Start a new session\n   107\t      await connector.handlers.session_start(ctx, { reason: \"new\" });\n   108\t      const state = connector.getState();\n   109\t      expect(state).not.toBeNull();\n   110\t      if (!state) return;\n   111\t      threadRef = state.threadRef;\n   112\t\n   113\t      // Capture content — a user prompt and assistant response (one turn)\n   114\t      // This queues derivation work (smoothed_prompt, turn_rendering, etc.)\n   115\t      await connector.handlers.message_end(ctx, {\n   116\t        message: makeUserMessage(\"please summarize this file\"),\n   117\t        entryId: \"entry-1\",\n   118\t      });\n   119\t      await connector.handlers.message_end(ctx, {\n   120\t        message: makeAssistantMessage({ text: \"here is the summary\" }),\n   121\t        entryId: \"entry-2\",\n   122\t      });\n   123\t      await connector.handlers.agent_end(ctx, {\n   124\t        messages: [makeUserMessage(), makeAssistantMessage({ text: \"here is the summary\" })],\n   125\t      });\n   126\t\n   127\t      // Get the instance and drain the background scheduler\n   128\t      const instance = connector.getInstance();\n   129\t      expect(instance).not.toBeNull();\n   130\t      if (!instance) return;\n   131\t\n   132\t      // Await drainSettled — all derivations complete or fail\n   133\t      await instance.sdk.drainSettled(threadRef);\n   134\t\n   135\t      // Query inspect.health for derivation outcomes\n   136\t      const health = await inspect.health(threadRef);\n   137\t      expect(health.ok).toBe(true);\n   138\t      if (!health.ok) return;\n   139\t\n   140\t      const report = health.value;\n   141\t\n   142\t      // Assert: at least one ready derived form exists\n   143\t      // Health report has owners array with counts per kind\n   144\t      const readyOwners = report.owners.filter((o) => o.counts.ready > 0);\n   145\t      expect(readyOwners.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/05-inference-host-routing.md | sed -n '20,125p'\"","aggregated_output":"    20\t- Classify provider errors into LHC failure kinds.\n    21\t- Prove a captured thread's queued derivation work runs through LHC's scheduler and records outcomes.\n    22\t\n    23\t**Scope Out:**\n    24\t\n    25\t- Assignment config loading and startup validation; owned by Story 6.\n    26\t- Derivation prompt and model dial-in quality tuning.\n    27\t- Context serving.\n    28\t\n    29\t**Dependencies:** Story 1 for initialized LHC instance. Story 2 for captured content used by AC-4.5.\n    30\t\n    31\t### Acceptance Criteria\n    32\t<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\n    33\t\n    34\t**AC-4.1:** The extension supplies one model-call function to the LHC instance at initialization. Given a (provider, model) pair and a system/user message list, the function resolves the pair through PI's model registry and auth and returns either the completion text or a classified failure.\n    35\t\n    36\t**TC-4.1** — The model-call function resolves a (provider, model) pair through PI's registry and returns completion text for a single-turn message list.\n    37\t\n    38\t**AC-4.2:** Different derivation kinds may route to different (provider, model) pairs within the same session. The function routes each call by its provided keys; LHC does not interpret the keys.\n    39\t\n    40\t**TC-4.2** — Two derivation kinds assigned to different (provider, model) pairs both route correctly in one session.\n    41\t\n    42\t**AC-4.3:** Provider errors map to LHC's failure classification: auth and invalid-request are terminal; rate-limit, timeout, and network are retryable. A thrown exception classifies as the generic `other` kind.\n    43\t\n    44\t**TC-4.3** — Auth and invalid-request map to terminal; rate-limit, timeout, network map to retryable; a thrown exception maps to `other`.\n    45\t\n    46\t**AC-4.4:** A call that resolves but produces no output is classified as `empty_output` by the LHC adapter. The host's function never returns `empty_output` itself — it returns text or a transport/auth failure.\n    47\t\n    48\t**TC-4.4** — A resolved call with no output is classified `empty_output` by the adapter; the host function returns text-or-transport-failure only.\n    49\t\n    50\t**AC-4.5:** A captured thread's background derivation work invokes the injected model-call function and records a result against the thread: a queued derivation runs through LHC's scheduler, calls the function, and persists either a ready derived form or a classified failure, queryable through inspect/health. This closes the loop end to end — capture to derivation to recorded outcome — not just the function in isolation.\n    51\t\n    52\t**TC-4.5** — A captured thread with queued derivation work, drained through LHC's scheduler with the injected function wired, persists at least one ready derived form (function returns text) and one classified failure (function returns a failure), both queryable through inspect/health — proving the capture→derivation→recorded-outcome loop, not just the function in isolation.\n    53\t\n    54\t### Technical Design\n    55\t<!-- Jira: Technical Notes or sub-section of Description -->\n    56\t\n    57\t#### Architecture Context\n    58\t\n    59\tStory 5 owns the host-supplied `ModelCall` function that lets LHC derivations use PI's existing model registry and auth. LHC passes provider/model keys and messages; the connector resolves those keys through PI and returns text or a classified failure.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe closed-loop proof is part of this story: a captured thread's queued derivation work must call the injected function and persist either a ready derived form or a classified failure.\n    62\t\n    63\t#### Build Strategy\n    64\t\n    65\tStrategy: full-staged-risk\n    66\t\n    67\tReason:\n    68\t- The runtime boundary and failure classification must match LHC's contract exactly.\n    69\t- The closed-loop check depends on real background scheduler behavior and captured content, not only the pure function.\n    70\t\n    71\tRisk Reminders:\n    72\t- The host function never returns `empty_output`; that classification is produced by LHC's adapter.\n    73\t- Different derivation kinds may route to different provider/model pairs in one run.\n    74\t\n    75\t#### Implementation Targets\n    76\t\n    77\t| Area | Files / Modules |\n    78\t|------|-----------------|\n    79\t| Model call host | `packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/model-call.ts` |\n    80\t| Failure classification | `packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/model-call.ts` |\n    81\t| Instance injection | `packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/instance.ts` |\n    82\t| Deterministic fakes | `packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.ts` |\n    83\t| Tests | `packages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts`, `packages/pi-lhc/test/inference/closed-loop.test.ts` |\n    84\t\n    85\t#### Design References\n    86\t\n    87\t- [epic.md §Flow 4](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:195), lines 195-207\n    88\t- [tech-design.md §External Contracts](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:102), lines 102-123\n    89\t- [tech-design.md §Module Responsibility Matrix](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:193), lines 193-197\n    90\t- [tech-design.md §Flow 4](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:357), lines 357-389\n    91\t- [tech-design.md §Architecture-Risk Tests](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:535), lines 535-549\n    92\t- [tech-design.md §Chunk 5](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:666), lines 666-668\n    93\t- [test-plan.md §Inference Tests](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:88), lines 88-102\n    94\t- [test-plan.md §Chunk 5](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:175), lines 175-180\n    95\t\n    96\t#### Test Mapping\n    97\t\n    98\t| TC | Test File / Check | Test Description |\n    99\t|----|-------------------|------------------|\n   100\t| TC-4.1 | `test/inference/model-call.test.ts` | Function resolves provider/model via PI registry and returns completion text for a single-turn message list. |\n   101\t| TC-4.2 | `test/inference/model-call.test.ts` | Two derivation kinds route to different provider/model pairs in one run and both succeed. |\n   102\t| TC-4.3 | `test/inference/model-call.test.ts` | Auth and invalid-request classify terminal; rate-limit, timeout, and network classify retryable; thrown exception maps to `other`. |\n   103\t| TC-4.4 | `test/inference/model-call.test.ts` | Resolved no-output completion becomes `empty_output` at the LHC adapter; host function returns text or transport/auth failure only. |\n   104\t| TC-4.5 | `test/inference/closed-loop.test.ts` | Captured thread's queued derivation invokes injected function and persists one ready form plus one classified failure queryable through inspect/health. |\n   105\t\n   106\t#### Architecture-Risk Tests\n   107\t\n   108\t| Risk | Test File / Check | Test Description | Why AC/TC Mapping Alone Would Miss It |\n   109\t|------|-------------------|------------------|---------------------------------------|\n   110\t| Adapter / Runtime Boundary | `test/inference/model-call.test.ts` | Each PI/pi-ai failure shape maps to the exact `ModelCallFailureKind`. | \"Classified failure\" can pass while individual runtime errors map to the wrong code. |\n   111\t| Concurrency / Lost Update | `test/inference/closed-loop.test.ts` | A stale background derivation result does not clobber newer thread state. | Closed-loop success does not prove background scheduler stale-result safety. |\n   112\t\n   113\t#### Technical Notes\n   114\t\n   115\t- `createModelCall(ctx)` resolves with `ctx.modelRegistry.find(provider, model)` and completes through PI's completion API.\n   116\t- Calls are single-turn: system/user messages only, no tools, streaming, or multi-turn local state.\n   117\t- TC coverage uses deterministic fakes, so auth availability never skips tests.\n   118\t\n   119\t#### Anti-Shim Requirements\n   120\t\n   121\t- Do not maintain a second credential store or provider registry.\n   122\t- Do not return placeholder text for failed derivations; return classified failures.\n   123\t- Do not prove the loop with a direct function call only; the scheduler must invoke the injected function on captured content.\n   124\t\n   125\t#### Production Path Proof\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
